window.WIKI_INDEX=[{"u": "index.html", "t": "Welcome to Wickhaven", "s": "Start here", "x": "The Chronicles of Thaleryn WICKHAVEN A frontier city on the coast, built over the bones of old Elaris. One flame was lit here in the dark and it has never gone out. Beyond the walls, the map is still blank. New here? Start here Command index Where to begin I New to Thaleryn How West Marches play works here, and how news travels. II Make a character Level 3, rolled stats, sheet settings, submission. III Walk the city Taverns, markets, shrines and the places you shouldn't go. IV Fill your days Gather, fish, mine, cook, craft and turn it all in. The four guilds Each guild runs its own district and sends one representative to the Conclave. None can decide for the whole city alone. The Wayfarers Waymeet · contracts, guides and the road out The Magisterium High Ward · knowledge kept, studied and guarded Hand & Hammer Hammerfell · the crafts and trade that run the city The Unbound The Drift · born with magic, answering to no one Heard at the Gilded Tankard Rumor The catch at the Pier is shrinking, and some fish carry strange pale markings. The harbor master has closed the northern shallows. Rumor Blue lights move between the trees past the eastern road after midnight, and vanish when anyone gets close. All rumors & mysteries → Beneath Lowside A collapsed wall revealed a tunnel to a buried temple of the Ancients. The tunnel is recent. Someone has been digging. Quest: THE RUINS · date to be announced The Ancients & Elaris → Adventuring Hunting Step through the wizard's portal into fourteen lands. The Blood Pit Steel meets steel beneath the roar of the crowd. Solo expeditions Head out alone with the Wickhaven Ledger. Reputation How the guilds see you, and what it opens up."}, {"u": "new-to-thaleryn.html", "t": "New to Thaleryn", "s": "Start here", "x": "How the world works The map shows only what players have found. Places a party discovers and routes they open can become permanent parts of Thaleryn's history, and a blank stretch of land can end up named after the party that went there. Everyone starts in Wickhaven. Your character can come from anywhere, but play begins in the city. You know what's public about it, not every neighborhood, guild, NPC or secret. The rest you learn in play. The tone is grounded and lived-in: wonder, mystery and a little coziness, with room for darker themes handled through the server's safety tools . Your first steps Read the rules and this wiki While your character is pending you're an Applicant : you can chat in general channels and read the quest board and session reports. Make your character Roll stats, build the sheet on D&D Beyond, and submit it. See Character creation . Get approved Staff review your sheet in a private thread. Once you're approved you become an Adventurer and the rest of the server opens up. Arrive at the City Gates Every newcomer arrives through the gates. A Gatewarden , an experienced player, can show your character around for their first two weeks. Find something to do Join a quest from the quest board, take a job , go hunting , try a solo expedition , or spend a quiet day on downtime . How characters learn things No metagaming Characters know only what they lived through in play or heard from session reports. Session reports News spreads the way it does in any frontier town: adventurers coming home and telling their stories. Parties can keep some secrets out of their reports. Rumors Anything in the Rumor Mill is something characters have heard , not a fact, until it's confirmed. See Rumors & mysteries . Secrets Things a party didn't find go into DM session notes, so another DM can pick up the thread later. Earning AP by writing The Wickhaven Ledger bot tracks AP for players with an approved character. You earn it by writing in character (1 AP per 300 words of Tupperbox posts in in-character channels, adding up across posts), by reaching milestones on solo expeditions , and once a day from !mine or a finished hunt. The cap is 4 AP a day . /ledger-ap shows where you stand. Register your Tupperbox brackets with /ledger-tupper register so the Ledger can match your posts to you."}, {"u": "character-creation.html", "t": "Character creation", "s": "Start here", "x": "Starting level All characters begin at level 3 , with starting equipment (not the starting gold option). Ability scores Set your sheet's ability scores to Manual / Rolled . Use !statrolls in the stat-roll channel. You get three sets. Pick one set and assign its scores to your abilities in any order. Don't mix scores from different sets. D&D Beyond sheet settings Setting Use Dice Rolling Enable Dice Rolling Optional Features Only Optional Class Features Advancement Type XP Hit Point Type Fixed Use Prerequisites Feats & Multiclass Requirements Level-Scaled Spells Show Level-Scaled Spells Encumbrance Use Encumbrance Ignore Coin Weight Enabled Ability Score Display Modifiers Top Privacy Public (required) . A private sheet can't be reviewed. Classes, species and backgrounds Allowed A 5.5e class with a legacy subclass A legacy class with a legacy subclass Mixing legacy and 5.5e species and backgrounds Not allowed A legacy class with a 5.5e subclass Multiclassing between legacy and 5.5e Anything that can't be built in D&D Beyond or run in Avrae Custom backgrounds and custom lineages need DM approval, case by case. Spells, feats and languages Spells: if a 5.5e version exists, use it. Legacy versions aren't allowed when an update exists. Feats: the same. Use the 5.5e version; a legacy feat is fine only if it has no 5.5e version. Languages: chosen by the rules of the source you built your character from. Submitting When you're ready, submit in the character submission channel with /ledger-character submit : your character's name, your D&D Beyond link, a link to your stat roll, and which set you chose. The Ledger opens a private review thread that only you and staff can see. A DM will tell you if anything needs changing. Players start with two character slots . More open up with time on the server, AP and active days. Retiring a character frees its slot."}, {"u": "table-safety.html", "t": "Table safety", "s": "Start here", "x": "Lines Never in the game Harm to children Sexual violence Animal cruelty Veils Fade to black in shared and public scenes Explicit sexual content Torture Content warnings Every quest post lists its heavy themes up top, so you can decide before you sign up. Death and consent Permanent character death only happens where the stakes were made clear beforehand: quests that say so, and places like the Blood Pit that are open about their risks. Even then, the city has people who know magic, alchemy, medicine and \"less conventional solutions to death.\" See Faiths & the Hush ."}, {"u": "wickhaven.html", "t": "Wickhaven and the Conclave", "s": "The world", "x": "Few people living today remember that the ground under Wickhaven once had another name: Elaris. The city Walls and the City Gates Every newcomer arrives through the gates, and every traveler is announced. The harbor and the Pier On the water side, bringing ships, merchants and goods. The Pier's waters have fed the city for generations. Farmland Fields and hedgerows outside the walls, slowly giving way to wilderness. The roads out Roads run from the city into the Outlands , the wild lands beyond the city's protection. Wickhaven is a frontier city : big enough for guilds, markets and a temple, young enough that the land past its farms is mostly unmapped. See Places of Wickhaven for where everything is. The Conclave Wickhaven is governed by the Conclave . Each of the four founding guilds sends one representative, and each guild runs its own district under its own rules. No guild can decide for the whole city alone, and their rivalries play out openly at the Conclave table. District Guild Known for Waymeet The Wayfarers Guild Contracts, companions, guides and rumors; where the city, the harbor and the roads meet High Ward The Magisterium Study, preservation and the responsible use of knowledge Hammerfell The Hand & Hammer The crafts and trade that keep the city running The Drift The Unbound Sorcerers and innate magic, answering to no one Lowside No guild Gambling, black markets, and something very old underneath Lowside has no guild and no Conclave seat. Who speaks for it, if anyone, is an open question. Players at the table The Conclave's seats will be held by actual players, chosen by players. Reputation decides who takes part: 625+ 🗳️ Trusted Name with a guild: you can vote 750+ 🪑 Notable with a guild: you can run for a seat How elections run is still being decided. See Reputation ."}, {"u": "places.html", "t": "Places of Wickhaven", "s": "The world", "x": "Town Square The City Gates Guards Where every traveler arrives and is announced. The Gatehouse Gatewardens Experienced players mentor new arrivals for two weeks, and their characters show newcomers around the city. The Gilded Tankard Tavern · Wren Ashdown Warm light, worn floorboards, a crackling hearth and a losing card game in the corner. Food, drink and rumors. !eat !drink !tavern rumor The Night Market After sundown Lantern-lit canvas stalls: common gear and uncommon magic. Not every shadow in the market belongs to a customer. !buy !sell !tools !learnrecipe Secondhand Goods A squinting shopkeeper with a ledger A cluttered pawn shop with a sticking door, dented helmets, a lute missing two strings, and a locked box nobody's opened. Buys ordinary gear at half its book price. !sell The Archive A portly archivist Towering shelves, chained ledgers, pages that turn with nobody there. \"The Archive has a long memory, and longer ears.\" !research · more The Wickhaven Lantern Inn · Odessa Varrow A cozy inn at the heart of the city, where adventurers rest and recover. Odessa lends out the kitchen. !lr !cook Hall of the First Flame Temple of the Wick · the Lamplighters The flame the founders lit over Elaris still burns here. Offerings, funerals, resurrection, and lifting the Hush. !offering !cleanse · more Lowside The city's underside. See Lowside for the full district. The Crooked Coin Down worn steps: smoke, dice in wooden cups, and a dealer whose smile never reaches his eyes. \"The dice are fair. Mostly.\" !gamble The Back Alley Poisons, forged papers, stolen goods. Nothing comes with a guarantee, and nobody will vouch for you if the guard finds out. !alley !remedy The Hollow Sanctum A buried temple of the Ancients, reached by a tunnel someone dug recently. Level 5+. !delve !bargain Outside the walls Place What's there Commands The Pier & the Beacon Shrine Fishermen, sailors and travelers; waters that have fed the city for generations. The Lamplighters' beacon stands at the end. !fish !gather forage !offering Farmland River-fed fields and hedgerows. Safe ground for new characters. !gather farm forage trap The Outlands roads Old roads past the last farm into deep woods. You can get hurt out here. !gather forage trap wood The Quarry & the Old Workings Worked for generations. Below it, abandoned tunnels break into old Elaris. !mine !dig The Hunting Fields A circle of ancient stones and a robed wizard's portal to fourteen lands. !hunt !skin · more The Workshop Ordinary (and not-so-ordinary) paid work, honest or otherwise, and every trade's benches. !job general !make !craft The Training Yard Slow, patient practice toward new proficiencies. !train · more The Blood Pit An arena with real consequences. \"The Pit will not save you. The city might.\" !bloodpit · more Looking for which command works where? See Where to go ."}, {"u": "the-ancients.html", "t": "The Ancients and Elaris", "s": "The world", "x": "Elaris The Ancients were an older civilization whose ruins lie beneath and around the city. Their city was Elaris , and Wickhaven was built on top of it. Few people living today remember the old name. Anything from the Ancients' era, from the Hollow Sanctum to old mile-markers and altered records, still carries it. History in Thaleryn is partly forgotten. Huge sections are missing, records contradict each other, and the same event can be remembered differently by different people. What's down there The Hollow Sanctum A collapsed wall in Lowside revealed a narrow hole and a deliberately dug tunnel leading to the remains of an ancient temple, buried and forgotten. The tunnel looks recent . Who dug it, and what else is down there? Quest: THE RUINS · date to be announced The Old Workings The abandoned lower tunnels beneath the Quarry, where they break into old Elaris. Rare metals, better gems, and real fights. Level 5+ with !mine deep . Finding Elaris yourself !dig at the Quarry or a charted ruin turns up relics of old Elaris , and sometimes a fragment of an Elaris recipe. !delve goes down into the Hollow Sanctum (level 5+). Deeper means better relics, worse traps and more Hush . !research at the Archive has two whole shelves on the subject: The Ancients and Beneath Elaris . See The Archive . Collect three fragments of an Elaris recipe and you can cook a dish nobody has made in centuries. See Cooking . Disturbing the old city can leave a mark: the Hush . See Faiths & the Hush ."}, {"u": "faiths.html", "t": "Faiths and the Hush", "s": "The world", "x": "The Wick When the founders raised Wickhaven over the ruins of Elaris, they lit one flame in the dark and swore it would never go out. Its priests, the Lamplighters , tend the First Flame, the street lamps, the Outlands road lanterns and the Pier beacon. They also handle funerals, resurrection, and lifting the Hush. Temple: the Hall of the First Flame, Town Square · Shrine: the Beacon Shrine, end of the Pier The Unlit The god the Ancients of Elaris served: keeper of the dark, the dead, and things meant to stay forgotten. Elaris sealed its dead and its secrets in the Unlit's keeping, and the First Flame was lit in defiance of it. Nobody admits to worshipping it, but someone recently dug a tunnel to its temple, the Hollow Sanctum . The Hush The Unlit's mark on anyone who disturbs what it keeps. It's a 10-bubble counter on your !hud . Digging can add 1 (2 on a rare find), delving adds 1–3, and a few encounters in the Old Workings add some too. At 10, your digs, delves and gathers are all made at disadvantage until it's lifted. Offerings and lifting the Hush Command Where Cost What happens !offering Hall of the First Flame 1 gp, once a day Kindled for 24 hours: +5 to find rarity on your next delve !offering Beacon Shrine, the Pier 1 gp, same daily use Fair Tide for 24 hours: +2 on your next !fish or shore gather !cleanse Hall of the First Flame 25 gp per point of Hush The Lamplighters lift some or all of it. Always works. !remedy The Back Alley 8 gp A street cure. It might clear it all, half, nothing, or make it worse. !bargain The Hollow Sanctum Relics as tribute (1 per 3 points of Hush) The Unlit lifts all of it, but keeps the best thing you find on your next delve. No second bargain until the debt is paid. You get one offering a day in total, at either shrine. Eating Founders' Bread also clears 1 Hush. Death and resurrection Resurrection exists in Thaleryn through temples, powerful NPCs and other means, always at a cost that depends on the situation. The city has no shortage of people who know magic, alchemy, medicine and less conventional solutions to death."}, {"u": "people.html", "t": "People of Wickhaven", "s": "The world", "x": "Wren Ashdown Barkeep · the Gilded Tankard Behind the bar at the Gilded Tankard, serving food, drinks and rumors. Odessa Varrow Innkeeper · the Wickhaven Lantern Keeps the city's coziest inn and lends out her kitchen. Mara Vey Townsperson Reported the blue lights moving past the eastern road three nights running. The Lamplighters Priests of the Wick · Hall of the First Flame Tend the First Flame, the lamps and lanterns, and the Pier beacon. The harbor master The harbor Says the pale fish are nothing, but closed the northern shallows anyway. The robed wizard The Hunting Fields Keeps the shimmering portal at the center of the stone circle. \"Choose carefully.\" The portly archivist The Archive Keeper of the chained ledgers. The Crooked Coin's dealer Lowside A smile that never reaches his eyes. The Secondhand Goods shopkeeper Town Square Squints, consults a ledger, and prices everything on sight."}, {"u": "rumors.html", "t": "Rumors and mysteries", "s": "The world", "x": "Something is wrong with the fish Rumor The catch at the Pier is shrinking, and some fish have strange pale markings. The harbor master says it's nothing, but has closed the northern shallows until further notice. The Magisterium pays well for Pale-Marked Fish Strange lights on the old road Rumor Three nights running, blue lights moving between the trees past the eastern road after midnight. They vanish when anyone gets close. Reported by Mara Vey Who dug the tunnel? A collapsed wall in Lowside opened onto a recently dug tunnel to the Hollow Sanctum, a buried temple of the Ancients. Someone has been down there. Quest: THE RUINS · date to be announced Hearing more !tavern rumor at the Gilded Tankard: what folk are whispering about. !research at the Archive has a Whispers & Rumors shelf, including The Blue Lights and The Pale-Marked Fish. See The Archive . Session reports carry news home from every party that goes out."}, {"u": "the-map.html", "t": "The map and the Outlands", "s": "The world", "x": "A map that grows The map shows the lands that have been charted, explored or documented, starting with Wickhaven. As quests unfold and players push past familiar ground, new places are found, forgotten ones are uncovered, and blank stretches become part of the map. Most importantly, you have a direct hand in shaping it. New locations are added through Map Update tickets and reviewed by the Conclave (staff). Places a party discovers and routes they open can become permanent parts of Thaleryn's history. A blank stretch of land can end up named after the party that went there. The Outlands The wild lands beyond the city's protection. Danger varies a lot by place: a quiet farm can sit a short ride from ruins, monsters and things that don't belong. The roads Old roads past the last farm into deep woods. Foraging, trapping and timber, with harder checks and real risk. Charted ruins Once a party maps a ruin, it becomes a place to !dig . The Hunting Fields Not the Outlands at all: a portal to lands far from Wickhaven. See Hunting . Still blank What the wider world beyond the Outlands looks like hasn't been charted yet. That's what the quest board is for."}, {"u": "waymeet.html", "t": "The Wayfarers Guild", "s": "Guilds & districts", "x": "The district of Waymeet The Wayfarers Guild The adventurers' guild. Waymeet sits where the city, the harbor and the Outlands roads meet. It never sleeps, and it's where contracts, companions, guides and rumors are found. Places in Waymeet Guildhall Contracts posted, parties formed, reports filed. Turn-ins, orders, pledges and jobs happen here. Business district Outfitters and guides for the road. Residences Home threads for characters who live here. Working with them How Command Rep Guild jobs on their board (guiding greenhorns, caravan escort) !job wayfarers +3, or +5 on a great result Turn in finds they want !turnin +1 / +3 / +10 by rarity Fill the weekly guild order !order deliver +10, plus 160% coin Finish a !craft project in their trade !craft +5 Paid lessons with their trainer !train +7 a session A guild won't hire or teach you below 125 rep (Despised). Rep from jobs counts toward the daily cap of 12; turn-ins, orders, lessons and finished crafts don't. Pledged to them? Everything counts ×1.5. Lore Guides, scouts and caravan riders who keep the roads open and the Outlands mapped. Example contracts from the guild's board have included an old, oddly well-kept road running north through thick woods, where no road was three weeks earlier. Proposed At a glance District Waymeet Turn-ins they want Most fish, hearty dishes for the inns, Golden Pumpkins and Golden Pheasants, road maps, compasses, waystones Their trades Cartography Their trainer teaches Simple weapons, cartographer's and navigator's tools, Athletics, Acrobatics, Animal Handling, Perception, Survival, Nature. Advanced: martial weapons Perks +5% when you sell at Secondhand Goods (375+) Conclave 🗳️ Vote at 625+ 🪑 Run for a seat at 750+ Other districts High Ward · Magisterium Hammerfell · Hand & Hammer The Drift · Unbound Lowside · no guild"}, {"u": "high-ward.html", "t": "The Magisterium", "s": "Guilds & districts", "x": "The district of High Ward The Magisterium A scholarly and arcane institution devoted to studying, preserving and responsibly using knowledge. Not a generic wizard school: historians, archivists, translators, alchemists, scribes and artificers all belong. Places in High Ward The Ashen Archive Proposed A restricted collection under one of the Magisterium's older wings: damaged, altered or partly lost knowledge. Dangerous or unreliable documents get a small ash-gray seal. Run informally by the Ashkeepers , who suspect some records were deliberately altered. Working with them How Command Rep Guild jobs on their board (copying manuscripts, cataloging finds) !job magisterium +3, or +5 on a great result Turn in finds they want !turnin +1 / +3 / +10 by rarity Fill the weekly guild order !order deliver +10, plus 160% coin Finish a !craft project in their trade !craft +5 Paid lessons with their trainer !train +7 a session A guild won't hire or teach you below 125 rep (Despised). Rep from jobs counts toward the daily cap of 12; turn-ins, orders, lessons and finished crafts don't. Pledged to them? Everything counts ×1.5. Lore The Magisterium keeps a careful distance from the Unbound, who don't think anyone should tell them what to do with magic they were born with. The Back Alley sells magic the Magisterium would rather keep locked away. At a glance District High Ward Turn-ins they want Ancient tablets and rune shards, Elaris mile-markers, Bluelight Caps, Pale-Marked Fish, strange monster parts, specimen fish Their trades Alchemy, calligraphy Their trainer teaches Common languages, calligrapher's supplies, Arcana, History, Investigation, Religion. Advanced: exotic languages, alchemist's supplies Perks None yet Conclave 🗳️ Vote at 625+ 🪑 Run for a seat at 750+ Other districts Waymeet · Wayfarers Hammerfell · Hand & Hammer The Drift · Unbound Lowside · no guild"}, {"u": "hammerfell.html", "t": "The Hand & Hammer", "s": "Guilds & districts", "x": "The district of Hammerfell The Hand & Hammer The working guild of crafts and trade that keeps the city running: loud workshops, crowded markets, carts and hammers. It oversees Hammerfell's businesses, homes and local rules. Places in Hammerfell Hammer's Rest Proposed A guildhall, workshop and common house wedged between bigger workshops, with smoke-darkened stone and a cart-wide door. One of the guild's best-known hangouts. Workshops and markets Loud, crowded and always busy. Working with them How Command Rep Guild jobs on their board (hauling stock, inspecting goods) !job hammer +3, or +5 on a great result Turn in finds they want !turnin +1 / +3 / +10 by rarity Fill the weekly guild order !order deliver +10, plus 160% coin Finish a !craft project in their trade !craft +5 Paid lessons with their trainer !train +7 a session A guild won't hire or teach you below 125 rep (Despised). Rep from jobs counts toward the daily cap of 12; turn-ins, orders, lessons and finished crafts don't. Pledged to them? Everything counts ×1.5. Lore Hand & Hammer crews work the Quarry, and you'll run into them while mining. Their standing decides your prices at the Night Market. At a glance District Hammerfell Turn-ins they want Hides, stone, ore, timber, old tools and fittings, shark hide, gar scales Their trades Smithing, woodworking (carpentry), leatherworking Their trainer teaches Artisan's tools, brewer's supplies, potter's tools, cook's utensils, Persuasion. Advanced: tinker's, glassblower's and jeweler's tools Perks Night Market: 5% off !buy and +10% on !sell at 375+; 10% off and +20% at 500+ Conclave 🗳️ Vote at 625+ 🪑 Run for a seat at 750+ Other districts Waymeet · Wayfarers High Ward · Magisterium The Drift · Unbound Lowside · no guild"}, {"u": "the-drift.html", "t": "The Unbound", "s": "Guilds & districts", "x": "The district of The Drift The Unbound A community guild of sorcerers and innate magic. Born with magic, they refuse to let anyone else decide what they do with it. Independent and eccentric. Places in The Drift The Ember Room Proposed A small, round, noticeably warm room in the Unbound guildhouse, scorch marks and all. Nobody officially named it. Working with them How Command Rep Guild jobs on their board (calming surging sorcerers, running messages) !job unbound +3, or +5 on a great result Turn in finds they want !turnin +1 / +3 / +10 by rarity Fill the weekly guild order !order deliver +10, plus 160% coin Finish a !craft project in their trade !craft +5 Paid lessons with their trainer !train +7 a session A guild won't hire or teach you below 125 rep (Despised). Rep from jobs counts toward the daily cap of 12; turn-ins, orders, lessons and finished crafts don't. Pledged to them? Everything counts ×1.5. Lore Performers, healers and wild talents who don't fit anywhere else. There's some tension with the Magisterium. The Unbound want anything born a little different, including fey, astral and elemental finds from the Hunting Fields . At a glance District The Drift Turn-ins they want Humming crystals, ember-glass, Wildfire Embers, Silverleaf, fey, astral and elemental finds, albino and golden fish Their trades Herbalism Their trainer teaches Instruments, painter's supplies, Medicine, Insight, Performance, Intimidation. Advanced: herbalism kit Perks None yet Conclave 🗳️ Vote at 625+ 🪑 Run for a seat at 750+ Other districts Waymeet · Wayfarers High Ward · Magisterium Hammerfell · Hand & Hammer Lowside · no guild"}, {"u": "lowside.html", "t": "Lowside", "s": "Guilds & districts", "x": "No guild · no Conclave seat Lowside The city's underside: gambling, black markets, and something very old underneath. Places in Lowside The Crooked Coin Down worn steps and through a sticking door: smoke, dice in wooden cups, and a dealer whose smile never reaches his eyes. \"The dice are fair. Mostly.\" The Back Alley Past the edge of Lowside: poisons, forged papers, stolen goods, and magic the Magisterium would rather keep locked away. The Crooked Garter A Lowside establishment for players who've opted in. Details to come. The Hollow Sanctum The buried temple below Lowside. See The Ancients . Lowside also has its own residences and business district. Gambling: !gamble Bets come straight out of your coin purse and winnings go right back in. 1–50 gp a bet, 5 plays a day (midnight UTC), only in the gambling channel. !gamble help shows your plays left. Game How it plays Example High-Low Call high or low , roll 2d6. A 7 always goes to the house. 8–10 or 4–6 pays even money, 11 or 3 pays 2 to 1, 12 or 2 pays 3 to 1. !gamble highlow 10 high Bones You and the house each roll 3d6. High roll wins even money. Ties cost half your bet. !gamble bones 5 Dragon's Eye Roll a d20. A natural 20 pays 12 to 1, 17–19 pays even money, anything else loses. !gamble eye 5 Cheating: add -cheat to roll Sleight of Hand against DC 15. Pass and you roll twice and keep the better result. Fail and you lose your bet and you're banned from the tables until midnight. The Back Alley: !alley Better gear than the town will sell you, one rarity higher, if you don't get caught. Getting caught costs you rep with the guild the item belongs to. Full rules on Losing rep & the Back Alley . The Alley also sells !remedy , an 8 gp cure for the Hush that might work. At a glance Guild None Who speaks for it? Nobody, at the Conclave. An open question. Commands !gamble !alley !remedy !delve !bargain The Lowside fixer A trainer with no guild: gaming sets, disguise kit, Deception, Sleight of Hand, Stealth; forgery kit, thieves' tools and poisoner's kit. Never checks rep, gives none. Other districts Waymeet · Wayfarers High Ward · Magisterium Hammerfell · Hand & Hammer The Drift · Unbound"}, {"u": "hunting.html", "t": "Hunting", "s": "Adventuring", "x": "\"Choose carefully.\" How a hunt works !hunt <biome> [difficulty] Rolls a random encounter for the biome channel you're in and adds the monsters to initiative. !huntmap Loads a battle map for that biome. Run it before !auto . Run it again to swap in a different map. !auto Places everyone on the map and runs the monsters. !skin Once the monsters are down and before the fight ends: harvest hides, bone and monster parts. Survival or Nature, DC 12, 5 stamina. !huntend Claims your XP and clears the combat. The fourteen grounds Each biome has its own channel, its own maps and its own finds. !huntmap only pulls maps for the channel you're in: no desert maps in the Arctic. Arctic !hunt arctic Astral !hunt astral Coastal !hunt coastal Swamp !hunt swamp Desert !hunt desert Feywild !hunt feywild Forest !hunt forest Grassland !hunt grassland Hill !hunt hill Mountain !hunt mountain Underdark !hunt underdark Haunted !hunt haunted Underwater !hunt underwater Urban !hunt urban Difficulty Leave it off for a random one, or name it: Easy Medium Hard Deadly (2014 rules) Low Moderate High (2024 rules) Hunting with friends? !hunt coastal -levels 2,3,4 sizes it for three players at levels 2, 3 and 4. No spaces after the commas. Map tools !huntmap list shows every map for the biome, and !huntmap grave finds Graveyard (partial names work). It uses croebh's Map Utilities , so !map and !move work as normal. !map help has the rest. Gathering in the biomes Every biome, its own list Frost lichen in the Arctic, fey dust in the Feywild, glowcaps in the Underdark. !gather forage , 8 stamina for a good haul. DC 13 to 15 Survival or Nature, depending on the biome. The harder biomes can hurt you on a bad roll. 56 to find Each biome hides at least one rare find. !journal wilds tracks them all. The Unbound want fey, astral and elemental finds; crafters use the rest. Encounter Generator by alpha983, monster data compiled by alij, lightly edited for the Chronicles of Thaleryn."}, {"u": "blood-pit.html", "t": "The Blood Pit", "s": "Adventuring", "x": "The Blood Pit is where adventurers come to test themselves, settle rivalries, sharpen their skills, or just see how long they can stay standing once things get real. Matches can be anything from controlled bouts between willing fighters to nasty fights against creatures dragged into the Pit for the challenge. The Pit doesn't care who you are. It only cares whether you can stand. The rules Rewards Tied to active participation; meaningful participation earns a full share. Defeat early or minimal activity may mean reduced rewards. If the whole party is defeated, no victory rewards. Rewards are split among the characters who took part. Level difference To keep matches reasonably balanced, the gap between the highest- and lowest-level character can't be more than 5 levels . Death The Blood Pit is not a simulation. Fail three death saving throws and your character dies by the normal rules. That doesn't have to be the end: if someone is willing to help, and you can afford it, a fallen companion may be brought back. See Faiths . Running a match Command What it does !bloodpit begin Begin a match !bloodpit start 7,8 Start an encounter (for example, CR 7–8) !bloodpit start 7,8 hard Start a harder encounter !bloodpit reward Finalize the rewards !bloodpit claim Claim your rewards !bloodpit end End the match !bloodpit end confirm Close the Blood Pit Claim your rewards before the combat session is ended or the Blood Pit is closed. The Blood Pit is a place to fight. It is not a place to throw away your character. Know the risks. Know your limits. Make it count."}, {"u": "solo-expeditions.html", "t": "Solo expeditions", "s": "Adventuring", "x": "How it works !depart <quest code> In the solo expeditions channel, for example !depart drowned-watch . /ledger-quests lists the quests and their codes; locked quests tell you how to unlock them. Check your departure Avrae posts your sheet (level, HP, skills, saves, ability scores, passive Perception), your coins and the bags you're carrying. The Ledger replies with a Departure Review. Look it over, then Confirm loadout . Play it through Make your choices with the buttons. Only you can press them. Come home You'll finish with Expedition Complete or Carried Home , plus AP for any new milestones. Reconcile If your HP, coins or items changed, the Ledger lists the changes. Update Avrae and your sheet, then run !depart reconcile <receipt-id> . New expeditions stay locked until this matches. What you carry By default you take your Pack, Belt, Potions and Scrolls bags. To pick different bags: !cvar ledger_carried_bags [\"Pack\", \"Belt\"] . Names must match !bag exactly. Your coin comes from your coin pouch if you're carrying it, or from your Avrae coin purse. Don't hide equipment you're actually carrying. Too much to fit? Carry fewer bags or ask staff. Before you go You need an approved character and a Bags Rewritten inventory. Run !bag once if you haven't. Every bag needs a unique name. If it says your sheet is missing something, run !update and try again. You can't depart at 0 HP, with an unreconciled expedition, or while another expedition is unfinished. /ledger-journal shows your visits and discoveries."}, {"u": "rest-and-recovery.html", "t": "Rest and recovery", "s": "Adventuring", "x": "!lr · Long rest Once every 24 hours. Only at the Wickhaven Lantern or in your own home thread under Residences. Refills your Tankard meals and home cooking. !sr · Short rest Twice every 24 hours, anywhere. Spend hit dice with !hd within an hour of it. !hd · Hit dice !hd shows your hit dice; !hd <number> spends that many. Multiclassed? !hd 2 d8 picks the die. Each die heals its roll plus your Constitution modifier. They come back on a long rest. Eating to heal Tankard meals: simple 1d4, hearty 2d4, one of each per long rest. Home-cooked dishes: twice per long rest, anywhere. See Cooking, eating & drinking . Keeping track !hud Your whole character on one card: HP, title, stamina, Hush, intoxication, blessings, digs and delves left, rep with every guild, journal progress, tools and meals. !cooldowns Every timer you've got: stamina, digs, delves, offerings, guild orders, rests, blessings, intoxication, the Hush and the weather. Daily limits reset at midnight UTC; weekly limits reset Monday at midnight UTC. See Timers & resets ."}, {"u": "reputation.html", "t": "Reputation guide", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Rep comes from the work you put in: turning in finds, taking guild jobs, training, crafting, filling guild orders and completing your collection journal. You can lose it too, mostly by getting caught in the Back Alley. Rep reflects how a guild sees your character, not how strong they are. Renown has nothing to do with power. Type !rep anywhere to see where you stand. The scale Despised Distrusted Newcomer Familiar Face Known Hand Trusted Name Notable Renowned 0 125 250 375 500 625 750 875 1,000 At a glance Starting rep 250 (Newcomer) with every guild Scale 0 to 1,000, in eight tiers of 125 Daily cap 12 rep per guild per day from everyday work. Resets at midnight UTC. Skips the cap Turn-ins, guild orders, training, finished crafting projects, journal pages Pledge bonus Rep you earn with your own guild counts ×1.5 The Conclave 625+ (Trusted Name) with a guild to vote, 750+ (Notable) to run for a seat Browse the guide Standing, tiers and the daily cap The four guilds Earning rep Pledging to a guild Guild jobs and training What standing unlocks Losing rep and the Back Alley"}, {"u": "rep-standing.html", "t": "Standing, tiers and the daily cap", "s": "Reputation", "x": "The eight tiers Each tier is 125 points wide. \"Renowned has nothing to do with power\": rep reflects how a guild sees your character, not their level. Despised Distrusted Newcomer Familiar Face Known Hand Trusted Name Notable Renowned 0 125 250 375 500 625 750 875 1,000 Rep Tier What it means 875+ Renowned A recognized name, with exclusive quests, personal storylines and unique resources. 750+ Notable Talked about outside your circle, with specialized quests and storylines. You can run for a seat on the Conclave. 625+ Trusted Name Part of the guild's ongoing story. Leadership and mentoring open up, and you can vote in the Conclave. 500+ Known Hand Trusted with bigger jobs. The best Hand & Hammer prices. 375+ Familiar Face Recognized, with personal NPC moments. Advanced lessons from the guild's trainer. 250+ Newcomer Where everyone starts. You can pledge to the guild. 125+ Distrusted Watched, with doors closing. You can't pledge, but the guild still hires you. 0+ Despised A real enemy of the guild. No jobs, no lessons, and recovery takes serious work. Checking your standing: !rep !rep works anywhere and shows your standing card: Your rep and tier with each guild How much rep you've earned with each guild today Who you're pledged to, if anyone Any rep loss still waiting to land (see Losing rep ) Any command that changes your rep also shows the result: your new total, the change, and a ⬆️ or ⬇️ when you move up or down a tier. The daily cap You can earn up to 12 rep per guild per day from everyday work. The cap is separate for each guild, so you could earn 12 with the Wayfarers and another 12 with the Unbound on the same day. It resets at midnight UTC. Counts toward the cap: guild jobs ( !job ) and guild encounters while mining ( !mine ). Skips the cap: turn-ins ( !turnin ), guild orders ( !order deliver ), training sessions ( !train ), finished crafting projects ( !craft ) and collection journal pages ( !journal ). If a gain would put you over the cap, you get whatever room is left and the result says Daily cap reached . Rep losses are never capped."}, {"u": "rep-guilds.html", "t": "The four guilds", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Guild Home Who they are 🧭 Wayfarers Waymeet Guides, scouts and caravan riders who keep the roads open and the Outlands mapped ✨ Magisterium High Ward Scholars, archivists and mages who study the Ancients and keep the records 🔨 Hand & Hammer Hammerfell Smiths, builders and tradesfolk who make, fix and sell what the city runs on 🔥 Unbound The Drift Performers, healers and wild talents who don't fit anywhere else Short names work in commands: wayfarers , magisterium , hammer , unbound . What each guild values Guild Turn-ins they want Crafting trades What their trainer teaches 🧭 Wayfarers Most fish, hearty dishes for the inns, Golden Pumpkins and Golden Pheasants, road maps, compasses, waystones Cartography ( !craft projects) Weapons, cartography and navigation, Athletics, Acrobatics, Animal Handling, Perception, Survival, Nature ✨ Magisterium Ancient tablets and rune shards, Elaris mile-markers, Bluelight Caps, Pale-Marked Fish, strange monster parts, specimen fish Alchemy, calligraphy Languages, calligraphy, alchemist's supplies, Arcana, History, Investigation, Religion 🔨 Hand & Hammer Hides, stone, ore, timber, old tools and fittings, shark hide, gar scales Smithing, woodworking, leatherworking Artisan's tools, Persuasion 🔥 Unbound Humming crystals, ember-glass, Wildfire Embers, Silverleaf, fey, astral and elemental finds, albino and golden fish Herbalism Instruments, painting, herbalism, Medicine, Insight, Performance, Intimidation Jewelcrafting has no guild yet. The Lowside fixer teaches games and shady kits and belongs to no guild. Guild perks Guild Perk Needs 🔨 Hand & Hammer Night Market: 5% off !buy 375+ (Familiar Face) 🔨 Hand & Hammer Night Market: 10% off !buy 500+ (Known Hand) 🔨 Hand & Hammer Night Market: +10% when you !sell 375+ (Familiar Face) 🔨 Hand & Hammer Night Market: +20% when you !sell 500+ (Known Hand) 🧭 Wayfarers Secondhand Goods: +5% when you !sell 375+ (Familiar Face) Any guild Advanced lessons from that guild's trainer 375+ (Familiar Face) Any guild Vote in the Conclave 625+ (Trusted Name) Any guild Run for a seat on the Conclave 750+ (Notable)"}, {"u": "rep-earning.html", "t": "Earning rep", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Command Where Rep Daily cap? Limit !turnin The guild's home Common +1, uncommon +3, rare +10 per item; delve relics double Skips None !order deliver The guild's home +10 Skips 1 per guild a week !job <guild> The guild's hall +3 on a pass, +5 on a great result Counts 2 jobs a day, 1 per board !train The trainer's channel +7 per paid session with a guild trainer Skips 1 session a week !craft Crafting channel +5 when you finish a project Skips 1 work session a day !journal Anywhere +15 when you complete a page, +25 more for the third full set Skips Once per page !mine The Quarry Some encounters earn rep with a guild Counts Stamina If you're pledged to a guild, every rep gain with that guild counts ×1.5 (rounded), capped or not. Turning in finds A find is worth either rep or coin, never both: turn it in at the guild that wants it, or sell it. !turnin list works anywhere and shows everything you're carrying that a guild wants. !turnin <item> [qty] or !turnin all in the guild's home hands it over. The Wayfarers also take hearty dishes for the inns at +1 rep each. See Turning in and selling for the full list. Guild orders Each guild posts one order a week, with new orders every Monday at midnight UTC: 5 of a common find or 2 of an uncommon one. !order shows all four. !order deliver in that guild's home pays 160% of the items' value in coin plus +10 rep . Jobs, training and crafting Guild jobs and training have their own page: Guild jobs and training . Crafting projects give rep with the guild that owns the trade; see Skinning and crafting . Your collection journal Each !journal page belongs to a guild. Find everything on a page for a title, +1 to that activity's rolls and +15 rep with the page's guild. Collect everything 3 times for a second title, +2 instead, and +25 more rep . See Stamina, tools and your journal . What doesn't give rep Offerings. !offering at the Wick's shrines gives a blessing, not guild rep. See The faiths . General work. !job general at the Workshop pays better coin and earns no rep. Selling. Anything you !sell earns coin only. The Lowside fixer. Lessons from a trainer with no guild don't give rep."}, {"u": "rep-pledging.html", "t": "Pledging to a guild", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Command What it does !pledge See who you're pledged to and when you can change it !pledge <guild> Pledge to a guild. Use it in that guild's home channel. !pledge <guild> confirm Switch guilds if you're already pledged !pledge leave See what leaving costs !pledge leave confirm Walk away from your guild Rules You need 250+ rep (Newcomer) with a guild to pledge to it. Pledges are made in person, in the guild's home channel. You can belong to one guild at a time. Rep you earn with your guild counts ×1.5 . That includes turn-ins, orders, training and crafting. The daily cap still applies to capped gains, so a pledge gets you to 12 faster but doesn't raise it. The bonus never applies to losses. Leaving or switching costs 25 rep with the guild you leave. After you pledge, switch or leave, you can't change your pledge again for 30 days . Examples !pledge magisterium !pledge hammer confirm !pledge leave confirm"}, {"u": "rep-jobs-and-training.html", "t": "Guild jobs and trainers", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Day labor: !job Poster by Sam. Guilds now hire from 125 rep. Take on a day's work around Wickhaven. !job shows today's postings on every board. !job <board> takes one, in that board's channel. You get 2 jobs a day , one per board. New postings go up at midnight UTC. Each job lists one or two skills and a DC. The alias rolls your best listed skill, or name one yourself: !job <board> <skill> (a partial name works, like !job hammer ath ). Name a skill the job doesn't use and you're told which ones it takes, and no job is used up. Beat the DC to pass. Beat it by 5 or more for a great result. A guild won't hire you below 125 rep (Despised). !job shows your odds to pass each posting and stars the two you're most likely to pass today. !job help shows the full instructions. !job general also answers to !job gen and !job workshop . Use a job in the wrong channel and it links you to the right one. Board Where Pays Rep general The Workshop Pass 2d4 gp, great 4d4 gp, fail 2d4 sp None wayfarers , magisterium , hammer , unbound That guild's hall Pass 1d4 gp, great 2d4 gp, fail nothing +3 pass, +5 great (counts toward the daily cap) Tell us what you did Jobs are a chance for a little character flavour, not just free coin. After your roll, add a line or two of RP about how it went: who you dealt with, what went wrong, what you overheard. Guild work fits the guild: Wayfarers guide greenhorns and ride caravan escort, the Magisterium copies manuscripts and catalogs finds, the Hand & Hammer hauls stock and inspects goods, and the Unbound calm surging sorcerers and run messages out of the Drift. Guild trainers: !train See also Training & study for the Training Yard and the Archive. Learn a new language, tool, weapon or skill from Wickhaven's trainers. It takes weeks of sessions and coin. Command What it does !train list What this trainer teaches, or where to find each trainer !train <proficiency> Start training, in that trainer's channel !train Take your weekly session (add adv , dis or guidance if they apply) !train status Your progress and when your next session is !train log Everything you've finished !train quit Drop your current training, with no refund One session a week. You pay, then roll d20 + your best ability for that proficiency: under 10 is no progress, 10–19 is +1, 20+ is +2. On a natural 1 the trainer sends you home and you don't pay. Sessions needed: 10 minus the ability modifier (15 for skills), minimum 3. One thing at a time. Cost: 5 gp a session for open lessons, 10 gp for skills and advanced lessons. Guild trainers give +7 rep per paid session, outside the daily cap. Advanced lessons need 375+ (Familiar Face) with the trainer's guild. If the guild Despises you, it won't teach you at all. The Lowside fixer belongs to no guild, never checks rep and gives no rep. When you finish, staff approve the new proficiency. Add it to your sheet and run !update . Trainer Open lessons Advanced (375+) ✨ Magisterium Common languages, calligrapher's supplies, Arcana, History, Investigation, Religion Exotic languages (Abyssal, Celestial, Deep Speech, Infernal, Primordial, Sylvan, Undercommon), alchemist's supplies 🔨 Hand & Hammer Carpenter's, mason's, smith's, cobbler's, leatherworker's, weaver's and woodcarver's tools; brewer's supplies, potter's tools, cook's utensils; Persuasion Tinker's, glassblower's and jeweler's tools 🧭 Wayfarers Simple weapons, cartographer's and navigator's tools, Athletics, Acrobatics, Animal Handling, Perception, Survival, Nature Martial weapons 🔥 Unbound Instruments, painter's supplies, Medicine, Insight, Performance, Intimidation Herbalism kit Lowside fixer Gaming sets, disguise kit, Deception, Sleight of Hand, Stealth Forgery kit, thieves' tools, poisoner's kit (no rep needed)"}, {"u": "rep-perks.html", "t": "What standing unlocks", "s": "Reputation", "x": "Rep Tier Unlocks Below 125 Despised No guild jobs and no lessons from that guild's trainer 125+ Distrusted Guild jobs open 250+ Newcomer Where everyone starts. You can pledge to the guild 375+ Familiar Face Advanced lessons from the guild's trainer; Hand & Hammer: 5% off at the Night Market and +10% when you sell there; Wayfarers: +5% when you sell at Secondhand Goods 500+ Known Hand Hand & Hammer: 10% off at the Night Market and +20% when you sell there 625+ Trusted Name A vote in the Conclave 750+ Notable You can run for a seat on the Conclave 875+ Renowned Exclusive quests, personal storylines and unique resources The Conclave The Conclave is Wickhaven's council. Its seats will be held by actual players, chosen by players. 🗳️ 625+ (Trusted Name) with a guild: you can vote. 🪑 750+ (Notable) with a guild: you can run for a seat. How elections run is still being decided. Watch the announcements."}, {"u": "rep-losing.html", "t": "Losing rep and the Back Alley", "s": "Reputation", "x": "What happened Rep lost With Caught in the Back Alley, but talked your way out 3–5 The guild the item belongs to Caught in the Back Alley and refused the bribe (or didn't answer) 10–15, or 20 on a natural 1 The guild the item belongs to Left or switched your pledge 25 The guild you left The Back Alley: !alley Past the edge of Lowside: poisons, forged papers, stolen goods, and magic the Magisterium would rather keep locked away. It sells better gear than the town will, but getting caught costs you with the guilds. Command What it does !alley See what's for sale at your level !alley buy <item> Try to buy something !alley pay Bribe the watcher after you're caught !alley refuse Keep your coin and take the rep hit Roll Stealth against the item's DC. Pass: you buy it and nobody knows. Fail, and you're caught. Roll Deception against the same DC. Pass: you keep the item and pay for it, but lose 3–5 rep . Fail the Deception roll and the item is confiscated with no money changing hands. Before the report goes in, you choose: !alley pay : a bribe of 50% of the item's price (100% on a natural 1). No rep lost, but the item is still gone. !alley refuse : take the hit, 10–15 rep (20 on a natural 1). Rarity DC Common 10 Uncommon 13 Rare 16 Very rare 19 Legendary 22 You get 3 tries a day . Checking the stock doesn't count, and neither does an item you can't afford. You need the full price on hand before you try. Back Alley prices run 25% over normal value. The Back Alley sells one rarity above what the town will sell you, and 🌑 marks those items. The town sells uncommon from level 1, rare from 5, very rare from 11 and legendary from 17. Each item shows the icon of the guild you'd lose rep with. Waiting losses The bribe offer stands for 10 minutes . Ignoring it doesn't dodge it: the loss lands the next time you use !rep , !pledge or !alley . Until then, !rep shows it as waiting. Climbing back There's no shortcut. Turn-ins and guild orders skip the daily cap, so they're the fastest way to rebuild. A guild that Despises you won't hire or teach you, which leaves finds, orders, crafting and your journal."}, {"u": "downtime.html", "t": "Downtime guide", "s": "Downtime", "x": "A day of downtime Every command on one table Stamina, tools & journal What every activity costs, and how to get better Where to go Which command works in which channel Each channel at a glance What every place looks like Gathering & fishing Farm, forage, trap, cut wood and cast a line Mining, digging & the Hush The Quarry, relics, the Sanctum and the Unlit's mark Cooking, eating & drinking Recipes, your Larder and the Gilded Tankard Skinning & crafting Turn finds into gear, potions and rep Training & study The Training Yard and the Archive The Archive Every research shelf and topic Turning in, selling & coin Rep or coin, never both Weather What the sky does to your odds Timers & resets When everything comes back Your first day Check !stamina and !weather . Head to the Farmland (safe) or the Pier and !gather or !fish . Run !turnin list to see who wants what you found, and turn it in at that guild's home for rep. Cook what's left at the Wickhaven Lantern, then !lr there. Lost? !hud shows your whole character and !cooldowns shows every timer."}, {"u": "quick-reference.html", "t": "A day of downtime at a glance", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Most downtime runs on stamina : 100 points that refill 1 every 12 minutes. Digging and delving have their own limits instead. Daily limits reset at midnight UTC. Command Where Cost or limit What you get !gather <farm, forage, trap or wood> Farmland, Outlands roads, the Pier, Hunting Fields 8 stamina (4 on a miss) Ingredients, materials, timber !fish The Pier 6 stamina a cast Fish, sometimes a snagged item !mine / !mine deep The Quarry 10 stamina (5 on a miss); deep is level 5+ Ore, stone, gems, coin, story encounters !dig The Quarry, charted ruins 2 a day Relics, stone, treasure, recipe fragments !delve The Hollow Sanctum (Lowside) 2 a week, level 5+ Better relics and treasure, more danger !skin After a hunt 5 stamina, once per fight Hides, bone, monster parts !cook <recipe> Your home, the Wickhaven Lantern 4 to 10 stamina per recipe Dishes for your Larder !make <recipe> Crafting channel 4 to 25 stamina per recipe Ingots, gear, potions, cut gems !eat , !drink The Gilded Tankard; Larder dishes anywhere Meals per long rest Healing, temp HP, XP, buffs !craft Crafting channel 1 work session a day Big projects, +5 guild rep !offering Hall of the First Flame, Beacon Shrine 1 a day A 24-hour blessing !turnin Each guild's home No limit Guild rep (skips the daily cap) !order deliver Each guild's home 1 per guild a week Coin and +10 rep !sell , !buy , !tools The Night Market No limit Coin, gear, recipe scrolls, tool upgrades !job The Workshop, guild halls 2 a day Coin, and rep for guild work !research The Archive 1 a day Scholar titles, tool and language training !gamble The Crooked Coin 5 a day Coin, maybe !alley The Back Alley 3 attempts a day Black-market goods When in doubt, !cooldowns shows every timer you have, and !hud shows your whole character on one card."}, {"u": "stamina-and-tools.html", "t": "Stamina, tools and your journal", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Stamina. You have 100, and it refills 1 point every 12 minutes, about 120 a day. Each activity costs a little. A natural 1 costs extra and a natural 20 refunds some. !stamina shows yours and what everything costs. Tools. Any basic tool for an activity in your bags gives +1 : a sickle for farming, a pickaxe for mining, smith's tools for smithing. Upgrade it at the Night Market with !tools buy <activity> : Tier Bonus Cost Unlocks Basic +1 Any tool in your bags Always Fine +2 300 gp After 50 uses of that activity Masterwork +3 1,500 gp Once that activity's journal page is complete The collection journal. !journal tracks every find, page by page: Harvest, Game, Catches, Timber, Minerals, Gems, Relics, Specimens, Wilds, Recipes and Treasures. Find everything on a page: a title, +1 to that activity's rolls, and +15 rep with the page's guild. Collect everything 3 times: a second title, +2 instead, and +25 more rep. Bad luck protection: after 25 finds in a row that you already have, the next one is guaranteed to be new. !journal title <title> picks which title shows on your !hud ."}, {"u": "locations.html", "t": "Where to go", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Place Commands Notes Farmland !gather farm , forage , trap Safe; good for new characters Outlands roads !gather forage , trap , wood Harder checks; you can get hurt Hunting Fields (14 biome channels) !gather forage , !hunt , !skin Each biome has its own finds The Pier !fish , !gather forage , !offering The Beacon Shrine is part of the Pier channel; offerings here give Fair Tide The Quarry !mine , !mine deep , !dig Ore, stone, gems, relics; the Old Workings at level 5+ Charted Outlands ruins !dig Added as parties map the Outlands The Hollow Sanctum (Lowside) !delve , !bargain Level 5+; opens after THE RUINS Residences (your home thread) !cook , !lr Your kitchen and your bed The Wickhaven Lantern !cook , !lr Shared kitchen and rooms The Gilded Tankard !eat , !drink , !tavern rumor Wren Ashdown's menu, and what folk are whispering about Hall of the First Flame !offering , !cleanse The Wick's temple Guild homes (4) !turnin , !order , !pledge , !job <guild> Each guild takes only what it wants The Workshop !job general General work Crafting channel !make , !craft Every trade The Night Market !buy , !sell , !tools , !learnrecipe Buys downtime finds, things you've made and treasure Secondhand Goods !sell Buys ordinary gear at half its book price The Back Alley !alley , !remedy Risky goods and a risky cure The Training Yard !train Weekly practice toward a new proficiency The Blood Pit !bloodpit Arena fights with real consequences The Archive !research Lore and Scholar titles The Crooked Coin !gamble High-Low, Bones and Dragon's Eye Anywhere !eat <dish> from your Larder, !sr , !hd , !rep , !bag , !recipes , !journal , !stamina , !weather , !cooldowns , !hud , !turnin list , !sell list , !order Checking and planning"}, {"u": "channels.html", "t": "Each channel at a glance", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Scene notes come from canon lore where it exists; rows marked (new) were added for the downtime build. For the full city guide, see Places of Wickhaven . Channel What happens here The scene Farmland Farming, foraging, trapping River-fed fields and hedgerows just outside the walls; stone walls, a millpond, beehives in hollow trees Outlands roads Foraging, trapping, woodcutting Old roads running past the last farm into deep woods; strange blue lights between the trees on certain nights Hunting Fields (14 biomes) Hunting, foraging, skinning A broad circle of ancient stones where a robed wizard keeps a shimmering portal to other lands The Pier Fishing, combing the shore, offerings at the Beacon Shrine A long pier over the water; fishermen and sailors, tide pools below, and a beacon at the end tended by the Lamplighters (the shrine is new) The Quarry Mining and digging Walls scarred by generations of pick and hammer; Hand & Hammer crews, carts and ledges. Below it, the Old Workings (new) : abandoned tunnels breaking into old Elaris The Hollow Sanctum (Lowside) Delving, bargaining with the Unlit The Hollow Sanctum: a buried temple of the Ancients under Lowside, reached through a collapsed wall and a recently dug tunnel. Faceless carvings, no lamps anywhere (details wait on THE RUINS) Residences Cooking, long rests Each player's own home thread The Wickhaven Lantern Cooking, long rests Odessa Varrow's cozy inn at the heart of the city, with a kitchen she lends out The Gilded Tankard Food, drink, rumors Warm light, worn floorboards, a crackling hearth, a losing card game in the corner, Wren Ashdown behind the bar Hall of the First Flame (new) Offerings, lifting the Hush The Wick's temple, where the flame the founders lit over Elaris still burns; tended by the Lamplighters. It stands in Town Square. Crafting Making and big projects Workshops of every trade: forges, benches, kilns, an alchemist's table The Night Market Buying, selling finds, tool upgrades, recipe scrolls Lantern-lit canvas stalls after sundown; not every shadow belongs to a customer Secondhand Goods Selling ordinary gear A cluttered pawn shop with a sticking door, dented helmets, a lute missing two strings, and a locked box nobody's opened The Back Alley Black-market goods, street remedies Past the edge of Lowside: poisons, forged papers, stolen goods The Workshop General jobs Ordinary (and not-so-ordinary) paid work, honest or otherwise Guild homes Turn-ins, guild orders, pledges, guild jobs Waymeet (Wayfarers), High Ward (Magisterium), Hammerfell (Hand & Hammer), the Drift (Unbound) The Training Yard Training Straw targets and practice dummies; slow, patient practice toward new proficiencies The Blood Pit Arena matches Sand, steel and a roaring crowd. The Pit will not save you. The city might. The Archive Research Towering shelves, chained ledgers, pages that turn with nobody there The Crooked Coin Gambling Down worn steps: smoke, dice in wooden cups, a dealer whose smile never reaches his eyes Where they sit. The Hall of the First Flame is in Town Square, with the Gilded Tankard, the Wickhaven Lantern, the Night Market, Secondhand Goods and the Archive. Lowside's channels are its residences, its business district, the Crooked Coin, the Crooked Garter, the Back Alley, and the Hollow Sanctum below."}, {"u": "gathering-and-fishing.html", "t": "Gathering and fishing", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Gathering Use !gather farm , !gather forage , !gather trap or !gather wood in the right channel. Here's how a gather works: You roll the better of the spot's two skills against its DC. Beat the DC for 1 find, by 5 for 2 finds, by 10 (or a natural 20) for 3. Your tool tier, journal bonus and the weather add to the roll. Any success has a 3–4% chance of a rare find. In risky spots, a very low roll costs you HP. Food goes to your Ingredients bag, everything else to Materials . Every find is logged in your journal. It costs 8 stamina on a find and 4 on a miss. A natural 1 costs 12; a natural 20 refunds 4. Spot Activities DC Risky? Rare finds Farmland Farm, forage, trap 12–13 No Golden Pumpkin, Silverleaf, Golden Pheasant The Pier Forage 11 No Tide Pearl Hunting Fields Forage 13–15 Harder biomes One per biome Outlands roads Forage, trap, wood 13–15 Yes Bluelight Cap, White Stag, Heartwood Being drunk or carrying a full Hush puts every gather at disadvantage. Fishing !fish casts into river and lake water, !fish sea toward open water. Each cast costs 6 stamina. Catches go in your Fish bag and tell you which guild wants them. Some casts snag gear, a potion, a gem, a scroll, or rarely a magic item instead. Your fishing rod, journal, the weather and the Fair Tide blessing all add to the roll. Watch for Pale-Marked Fish ; the Magisterium pays well for them (and see Rumors & mysteries ). Sea fog helps the fishing; gales and storms don't. See Weather . Make an !offering at the Beacon Shrine first for Fair Tide : +2 on your next cast or shore gather. Who wants your catch Wayfarers Food for the inns and the harbor Hand & Hammer Hides, scales and shell for the workshops Magisterium Strange specimens for study Unbound Anything born a little different !turnin list shows what you're carrying and who wants it. Turn it in at that guild's home."}, {"u": "excavation.html", "t": "Mining, digging, delving and the Hush", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Mining !mine at the Quarry, 10 stamina (5 when you come up empty). Each run draws one of 141 encounters from alpha983's Mining sheet, reworked for Wickhaven: ore veins, stone, gems, Hand & Hammer crews, lost kobolds, a playful mimic, ambushes. Many ask for a check or a save. Coin goes straight into your purse. Ore and stone go to Materials , gems to Gems , potions and scrolls into their bags. Failures can cost HP. A few encounters belong to the Unlit and can add Hush. !mine deep (level 5+) goes into the Old Workings , the abandoned lower tunnels that break into old Elaris. That's where the real fights, platinum, mithral, adamantine, high-value gems and uncut stones are, plus a 10% chance of treasure on a success. A pickaxe helps; storms hurt. Digging !dig , twice a day at the Quarry or a charted Outlands ruin. You roll Athletics: 10+ for 1 find, 15+ for 2, 20+ for 3. Every success also turns up 1–2 Building Stone, Iron Ore or Copper Ore. Finds are relics: mostly common, sometimes uncommon, rarely rare. There's a 25% chance of treasure (a gem, potion, scroll or magic item) and a small chance of an Elaris recipe fragment. A roll under 8 hurts (1d4). There's a 10% chance per dig of gaining Hush. Delving !delve , twice a week, level 5+, in the Hollow Sanctum, the temple under Lowside. A second roll decides how deep you get: Depth Treasure chance Trap damage Hush chance Outer chambers 40% 1d6 15% Deeper halls 60% 2d6 25% Sealed sanctum 90% 3d6 40% Relics from a delve are worth double at turn-in. The depth names will change once THE RUINS has been played. The Hush The Hush is the mark of the Unlit, a 10-bubble counter shown on !hud . Digging can add 1 (2 on a rare find); delving adds 1–3. At 10, your digs, delves and gathers are all made at disadvantage until it's lifted. See Faiths & the Hush for the three ways to clear it, or eat Founders' Bread to clear 1."}, {"u": "food-and-drink.html", "t": "Cooking, eating and drinking", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Learning recipes Everyone knows four basic recipes. Buy more as scrolls at the Night Market (200–1,000 gp), then !learnrecipe <name> . The four Elaris recipes need 3 fragments each from digging or delving, or a Trades & Crafts Scholar title from !research . !recipes shows what you know and what each dish needs. Cooking !cook <recipe> in your own home thread or the Wickhaven Lantern's kitchen. Recipes ask for food types (any grain, any herb) or a named ingredient. Rare finds are never used up for a generic type. The check is DC 10 for basic recipes, 12 for scroll recipes and 15 for Elaris recipes; cook's utensils give +2. Finished dishes go in your Larder . Eating !eat <dish> eats from your Larder anywhere. Food Where Effect Limit Tankard snack Gilded Tankard Flavor only None Tankard simple meal Gilded Tankard Heals 1d4 1 per long rest Tankard hearty meal Gilded Tankard Heals 2d4 1 per long rest Home-cooked dish Anywhere Heals 1d4 (simple) or 2d4 (hearty) 2 per long rest Founders' Bread Anywhere Removes 1 Hush No counter Lamplighter's Feast Anywhere 1d8 temp HP No counter The Scholar's Supper Anywhere 25 XP per level No counter White Stag Roast Anywhere Heals 3d4, 1d8 temp HP No counter The Wayfarers also take hearty dishes at their guild home for the inns: +1 rep each. Drinking !drink at the Gilded Tankard. Each drink adds 1–4 Intoxication (max 7). At 5 you're drunk: gathering, digging, delving and gambling are at disadvantage. It wears off at 1 point every 2 hours."}, {"u": "skinning-and-crafting.html", "t": "Skinning and crafting", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Skinning !skin after a hunt is over, before combat ends (before !i end ). It costs 5 stamina, once per character per fight. Survival or Nature against DC 12; a dagger or other blade adds +2. What you get depends on the creature: beasts give hide, bone and sinew; monsters can give fangs, venom sacs, strange hides, ichor, even dragon scales. Everything goes to Materials . Making (quick, from materials) !recipes <trade> lists a trade's recipes, marked ✅ if you can make them right now; !recipes ready shows everything you can make. Then !make <recipe> in the crafting channel turns materials into finished goods on the spot. There are six trades, and you need each trade's tools in your bags. Trade Ability Makes Guild for !craft projects Smithing Strength Iron, copper, bronze, silver, gold, steel, mithral and adamantine ingots; daggers, sickles, pickaxes, traps, longswords, chain shirts Hand & Hammer Woodworking Dexterity Planks, quarterstaffs, fishing rods, arrows, shields, bows Hand & Hammer Leatherworking Dexterity Tanned leather, pouches, waterskins, backpacks, leather and studded leather armor Hand & Hammer Herbalism Wisdom Potions of Healing and Greater Healing, antitoxin Unbound Alchemy Intelligence Oil, perfume, acid, alchemist's fire, climbing and water breathing potions Magisterium Jewelcrafting Intelligence Cut gems (worth three times the uncut stone), silver lockets, gold rings None yet Roll the trade's ability, plus your proficiency bonus if you add prof , plus your tool tier and journal bonus. Each recipe lists its DC and stamina in !recipes <trade> . Beat the DC by 10 for an extra one. The more different recipes you've made, the better your chance of a bonus item (15% at 10 recipes, up to 35%). A natural 1 ruins the materials; any other miss keeps them and costs half the stamina. Real items go into your bags. Add them to your D&D Beyond sheet. !sell only buys back things you actually made. Big projects (slow, coin) !craft is for big projects worked one day at a time at the Workshop. You pay for the materials up front. Command What it does !craft list What you can make, with cost, days of work and DC !craft <item> Start a project (one at a time) !craft work Put in one day of work; add prof if you're proficient with the tool !craft See your current project !craft quit Abandon it. The materials are lost. One work session a day: d20 + the tool's ability (+ proficiency) against the DC. Pass for +1 day of progress, beat it by 5 for +2, and a natural 1 makes no progress. Tools you learned with !train count as proficient automatically. Finishing a project gives +5 rep with the guild that owns the trade, outside the daily cap: Hand & Hammer for smithing, leatherworking and carpentry; the Magisterium for alchemy and calligraphy; the Unbound for herbalism; the Wayfarers for cartography. The finished item goes into your bags. Add it to your D&D Beyond sheet."}, {"u": "training-and-study.html", "t": "Training and study", "s": "Downtime", "x": "There are two ways to pick up something new between scenes: practice at the Training Yard, or study at the Archive. The Training Yard · !train The Archive · !research How often Once a week per character Once a day per character Teaches Languages, tools, weapons and skills Tools, instruments and languages (Trades & Crafts, Tongues & Tunes shelves) Finish line A set number of sessions; !train log shows your progress 20 successful researches in that topic Rep +7 per paid session with a guild trainer None Also gives Advanced lessons at 375+ with the trainer's guild Scholar titles on lore topics (10 successes) The Training Yard !train list shows what you can train right now. !train <proficiency> starts something new; !train picks up where you left off. Add adv or dis when they apply. !train help lists every command and option. You can start or continue training once per week . It stands for real time spent practicing, so the weekly limit can't normally be skipped. Progress takes time. Find a teacher or a sparring partner, practice, and come back next week. Guild trainers, their lessons and costs are on Guild jobs & trainers . Show up, put in the work, and come back next week. The Archive !research \"<topic>\" \"<skill>\" , once a day. Training topics take 20 successes to complete a tool, instrument or language, and a DM adds it to your sheet. Full topic list on The Archive . Tools you learned with !train count as proficient automatically for !craft ."}, {"u": "the-archive.html", "t": "The Archive", "s": "Downtime", "x": "How research works !research \"<topic>\" \"<skill>\" , for example !research \"Lock Pick\" \"Sleight of Hand\" . !research on its own shows the shelves and how much research you have left today; !research progress shows your studies. Roll a d20 against the topic's DC. Most topics are DC 11 to 13; deeper mysteries go up to 16. Advantage if the skill you name fits the topic and you're proficient in it. Otherwise it's a straight roll. The skill is optional. Think about how your character researches. Reading old records? History. Asking around the taverns? Persuasion. Poking around cellars? Investigation. Topic names are forgiving: capitals, spaces and dashes don't matter, and close misses get a \"Did you mean…?\" 1 research a day , tracked on your Research counter. It resets at midnight UTC. A typo or a topic the Archive doesn't have won't use it up. 10 successes on a lore topic make you a Scholar of it. A DM may give you advantage when that knowledge matters. 20 successes on a training topic complete that tool, instrument or language. A DM adds it to your sheet. A success might turn up something worth bragging about, a lead worth chasing, or something that makes you laugh. If it sounds like the start of a story, bring it to a DM or into RP. Skills never improve through research; they only decide whether you roll with advantage. The lore shelves The Ancients Ancient Artifacts, Ancient Inscriptions, Ancient Roads, The Ancients, The City That Never Slept, The Last King, The Star Doors, The Vanishing Beneath Elaris Construction Finds, Old Foundations, Sealed Doorways, The Deep Stair, The Repeated Symbol, Tunnels & Passages The Guilds & the Conclave Guild Rivalries, Sorcerous Bloodlines, The Conclave, The Deeper Halls, The Hand & Hammer, The Hard Winter, The Magisterium, The Unbound, The Wayfarers Guild Streets of Elaris Customs & Traditions, Do Not Leave the City, Faith & Shrines, Festivals, Local History, Lowside, Taverns & Inns, The City Guard, The Harbor, The Night Market The Road & the Outlands Abandoned Settlements, Maps & Blank Spaces, Missing Travelers, Outlands Beasts, The Eastern Road, The Outlands Whispers & Rumors Tavern Gossip, The Blue Lights, The Pale-Marked Fish Arcana & the Wild Cursed Objects, Dangerous Plants, Dragons, Everyday Magic, Forbidden Magic, Rare Herbs, Strange Dreams, Strange Weather, The Fey, The Restless Dead Trades & Crafts Topic Trains Alchemy Alchemist's Supplies Brewing Brewer's Supplies Cartography Cartographer's Tools Cooking Cook's Utensils Disguise Disguise Kit Forgery Forgery Kit Games of Chance Dice Set Herbalism Herbalism Kit Lock Picking Thieves' Tools Navigation Navigator's Tools Smithing Smith's Tools Stonework Mason's Tools Woodworking Carpenter's Tools Tongues & Tunes Languages: Draconic Dwarvish Elvish Infernal Sylvan Instruments: Drum Flute Lute New topics show up on the shelves over time. !research always has the current list. Need an extra research? Ask a DM."}, {"u": "trade.html", "t": "Turning in, selling and coin", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Turning in !turnin at a guild's home channel. Rep depends on rarity: common +1, uncommon +3, rare +10. Delve relics are worth double. Turn-ins skip the daily rep cap, and a pledge multiplies them by 1.5. !turnin list (works anywhere) shows everything you're carrying that a guild wants, and !turnin all hands over everything that guild takes. Guild What they want Wayfarers Most fish, hearty dishes for the inns, Golden Pumpkins and Golden Pheasants, road maps, compasses, waystones Hand & Hammer Hides, stone, ore, timber, old tools and fittings, shark hide, gar scales Magisterium Ancient tablets and rune shards, Elaris mile-markers, Bluelight Caps, Pale-Marked Fish, strange monster parts, specimen fish Unbound Humming crystals, ember-glass, Wildfire Embers, Silverleaf, fey, astral and elemental finds, albino and golden fish Selling !sell works in two shops, and they buy different things: Shop Buys Pays Guild bonus The Night Market Downtime finds (crops, game, fish, ore, stone, gems, relics, parts), things you made with !cook or !make , treasure you found, and gear you bought there Each find's listed price; Fine and Excellent goods sell for more; bought gear at half price Hand & Hammer: +10% at 375 rep, +20% at 500 Secondhand Goods Ordinary gear: anything with a book price in the shop list, wherever it came from Half the book price Wayfarers: +5% at 375 rep !sell list shows what the shop you're standing in will buy and for how much. !sell only buys back things a character actually made or found, so starting gear and D&D Beyond items can't be passed off as crafted goods or treasure. Remove whatever you sell from your D&D Beyond sheet. Buying !buy at the Night Market. Prices follow the 2024 Player's Handbook. !buy shows the stalls ( general , arms , armor , tools , arcana , apothecary , stables ), !buy <stall> shows one, and !buy <item> [qty] buys up to 100 at a time. Recipe scrolls ( !learnrecipe ) and tool upgrades ( !tools buy ) are sold there too. Hand & Hammer standing gets you 5% off at 375+ and 10% off at 500+ . What you buy goes into your bags. Add it to your D&D Beyond sheet too. Anything you buy can be sold back with !sell . Guild orders Each guild posts one order a week (new orders every Monday at midnight UTC): 5 of a common find or 2 of an uncommon one. !order shows all four. !order deliver in that guild's home fills it for 160% of the items' value in coin plus +10 rep , outside the daily cap. One delivery per guild per week. Other ways to earn coin Day labor !job : 2 jobs a day. General work at the Workshop pays better coin; guild work pays less but earns rep. More The Crooked Coin !gamble : High-Low, Bones or Dragon's Eye, 1–50 gp a bet, 5 plays a day. More The Back Alley !alley : better gear than the town sells, if you don't get caught. More"}, {"u": "weather.html", "t": "Weather", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Weather How often What it changes Clear Skies Often Nothing Overcast Often +2 trapping on the Outlands roads Rain Often +1 find farming and foraging in the Farmland Sea Fog Sometimes +2 on !fish and Pier gathers Summer Heat Sometimes −2 farming and Outlands trapping Gale Sometimes −2 on !fish ; double rare finds on the Pier shore First Frost Rarely −2 farming; double rare finds foraging the Farmland Storm Rarely −2 on !fish and on digging at the Quarry Blue Lights Very rarely Triple rare finds foraging the Outlands roads"}, {"u": "timers-and-resets.html", "t": "Timers and resets", "s": "Downtime", "x": "Other downtime !job : 2 jobs a day, general work at the Workshop or guild work at a guild hall (Distrusted or better). Pays coin, and guild jobs give 3–5 rep. !research : daily study at the Archive . 10 successes on a lore topic earns a Scholar title. !train : weekly lessons toward new proficiencies. See Training & study . !gamble : 5 plays a day at the Crooked Coin. !alley : 3 tries a day to buy from the Back Alley without getting caught. !hunt : hunts in the Hunting Fields, alone or with a party. See Hunting . !bloodpit : arena matches. See The Blood Pit . Resting !lr once per 24 hours at the Wickhaven Lantern or your own home. !sr twice per 24 hours. !hd spends hit dice within an hour of a short rest. Tavern meals and home cooking refill on a long rest. More on Rest & recovery . When things reset Timer Resets Stamina 1 point every 12 minutes, up to 100 Digs, jobs, gambling, research, Back Alley tries, offerings, crafting work, the daily rep cap Midnight UTC Delves, guild orders, training Weekly (Monday, midnight UTC) Long and short rests 24 hours after you take them Meal counters Your next long rest Blessings 24 hours after the offering, or when used Weather Every 6 hours Intoxication 1 point every 2 hours"}, {"u": "commands.html", "t": "Command index", "s": "Reference", "x": "All Downtime Core Hunting Server aliases Command What it does Where Limit More !alley Buy from the Back Alley; risk getting caught The Back Alley 3 tries a day Guide !bargain Ask the Unlit to lift your Hush The Hollow Sanctum Until the debt is paid Guide !buy Shop the Night Market stalls The Night Market — Guide !cleanse The Lamplighters lift the Hush, 25 gp a point Hall of the First Flame — Guide !cook Cook a dish from your ingredients Your home thread, the Lantern 4–10 stamina Guide !craft Multi-day projects at the Workshop Crafting channel 1 session a day Guide !delve Go down into the temple under Lowside (level 5+) The Hollow Sanctum 2 a week Guide !depart Solo expedition with the Wickhaven Ledger Solo expeditions — Guide !dig Dig for relics of old Elaris The Quarry, charted ruins 2 a day Guide !drink Drinks at the Gilded Tankard The Gilded Tankard Intoxication Guide !eat Tankard meals, or dishes from your Larder Tankard / anywhere Per long rest Guide !fish Fish off the Pier The Pier 6 stamina a cast Guide !gamble High-Low, Bones, Dragon's Eye The Crooked Coin 5 a day Guide !gather Farm, forage, trap or cut wood Farmland, roads, Pier, biomes 4–12 stamina Guide !job A day's work for coin, or guild rep The Workshop, guild halls 2 a day Guide !journal Your collection journal and titles Anywhere — Guide !learnrecipe Learn scroll or Elaris recipes Night Market / anywhere — Guide !make Ingots, gear, potions, cut gems Crafting channel Stamina Guide !mine Mine the Quarry (deep at level 5+) The Quarry 5–10 stamina Guide !offering 1 gp to the Wick for Kindled or Fair Tide Hall / Beacon Shrine 1 a day Guide !order Weekly guild orders Guild homes 1 per guild a week Guide !recipes Your recipe book Anywhere — Guide !remedy A cheap, risky cure for the Hush (8 gp) The Back Alley — Guide !research Study a topic at the Archive The Archive 1 a day Guide !sell Sell gear and finds Night Market, Secondhand Goods — Guide !skin Harvest parts after a fight Hunting channels 1 a fight, 5 stamina Guide !stamina Your stamina and what things cost Anywhere — Guide !tools Tool tiers and upgrades Anywhere / Night Market — Guide !turnin Hand finds to a guild for rep Guild homes (list anywhere) — Guide !weather Today's weather and its effects Anywhere Changes every 6 hours Guide !cooldowns Every timer you've got Anywhere — Guide !hd Spend hit dice after a short rest Anywhere Within 1 hour of !sr Guide !hud Your whole character on one card Anywhere — Guide !lr Long rest The Lantern or your home 1 every 24 hours Guide !rep Your standing with all four guilds Anywhere — Guide !sr Short rest Anywhere 2 every 24 hours Guide !hunt A random encounter for your biome Hunting Fields biomes — Guide !huntmap A battle map for your biome Hunting Fields biomes — Guide !huntend Claim XP and clear the fight Hunting Fields biomes — Guide !bloodpit Run an arena match The Blood Pit — Guide !train Weekly training toward a proficiency Training Yard, trainers 1 a week Guide !pledge Join, switch or leave a guild Guild homes 30 days between changes Guide !tavern rumor What folk are whispering about The Gilded Tankard — Guide !statrolls Roll your three stat sets Stat-roll channel — Guide Downtime , Core and Hunting are the Thaleryn Workshop collections. Server aliases are set up on the server separately."}, {"u": "glossary.html", "t": "Glossary", "s": "Reference", "x": "Term Meaning AP Points the Wickhaven Ledger gives for writing in character, solo-expedition milestones and daily gathering. Up to 4 a day. More Adventurer The role for players with an approved character. More Applicant The role for new joiners whose character isn't approved yet. More The Ancients The older civilization whose city, Elaris, lies under Wickhaven. More The Conclave Wickhaven's governing council: one seat per founding guild. Out of character, also the staff group that approves lore. More Daily cap The most rep you can earn per guild per day from everyday work: 12. More Elaris The Ancients' city, buried under Wickhaven. Also the name on Elaris recipes and relics. More Elaris recipe A dish rebuilt from 3 fragments found by digging, delving or mining. More Fair Tide A 24-hour blessing from an offering at the Beacon Shrine: +2 on your next cast or shore gather. More Gatewarden An experienced player who mentors a new arrival for two weeks. More The Hush The Unlit's mark. Fills to 10 from digging and delving; at 10 your digs, delves and gathers are at disadvantage. More Intoxication Goes up 1–4 a drink, max 7. At 5 you're drunk. Wears off 1 point every 2 hours. More Kindled A 24-hour blessing from an offering at the Hall of the First Flame: +5 to find rarity on your next delve. More The Lamplighters Priests of the Wick. More Larder The bag your cooked dishes go into. Eat from it anywhere. More Masterwork The top tool tier (+3), unlocked by completing that activity's journal page. More Pledge Formally joining one guild. Rep with them counts ×1.5. More Rep Reputation: your standing with each guild, 0 to 1,000. More Scholar A title for 10 successful researches on one lore topic. More Session report A party's account of a quest. How news travels in Wickhaven. More Stamina 100 points that refill 1 every 12 minutes. Most downtime costs some. More The Unlit The buried god of the Ancients: keeper of the dark, the dead and things meant to stay forgotten. More The Wick Wickhaven's public faith, built around the First Flame. More The Wickhaven Ledger The server's bot: character submissions, AP, solo expeditions and quest feedback. More"}];
